# mm.mcu

A portable microcontroller hardware interface, in partitions by facility. It is
pure C++: no foreign header, no C linkage, and no implementation of any facility.

A platform supplies an object rather than functions. A name declared in this
module's purview is attached to it and cannot be defined from outside, so the seam
is a Platform subclass the platform's own module registers when it is linked in.
Whatever C the platform needs lives on its side of that seam.

platform-interface is the marker that makes the selected platform's provider part
of an executable's link closure, and only of the executables whose closure reaches
this module. The marker takes no value: module: above already names the interface,
and a second spelling of one name is a mismatch waiting to be diagnosed. Compiling,
checking, modelling, and documenting this module need no platform at all; a
provider becomes a requirement when an executable that reaches it is linked.

Partitions are listed before the primary interface because source order is
declared, and the primary re-exports them.
